        <div id="why-no-battery-heater" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why a True Battery Space Heater Barely Exists
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heating a Room Takes Real Power</h3>

          <p>
            Every few winters a wave of products appears promising a cordless, rechargeable space heater that warms a room for hours. They do not work, and the reason is not a lack of engineering effort. It is physics. Electric resistance heat is close to 100 percent efficient, which sounds good until you realize it means one watt of electricity becomes exactly one watt of heat and no more. A standard plug-in heater draws 1,500 watts on high because that is roughly what it takes to warm a typical room in cold weather.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Batteries Actually Hold</h3>

          <p>
            Now look at batteries. A large cordless tool battery holds about 100 to 150 watt-hours. A heated vest pack holds around 35. A big laptop-style power bank holds under 100. Run any of those at 1,500 watts and it is empty in somewhere between one and six minutes, assuming the battery could even deliver that much current, which most cannot. To run a 1,500W heater for a single eight-hour night, you would need about 12,000 watt-hours of battery, which is the size of a whole-home backup system costing thousands of dollars.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Honest and Dishonest Battery Heaters</h3>

          <p>
            That is why the products that claim to be battery space heaters fall into two groups. The honest ones use a battery to run a fan or igniter while propane does the heating. The dishonest ones are tiny USB or battery heaters that put out 10 to 50 watts and are marketed with photos of cozy living rooms. A 30W heater is a hand warmer with a fan. It will not change the temperature of a room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers That Matter</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,500W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Draw of a standard space heater on high</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~5 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Runtime of a 5Ah 18V tool battery at 1,500W, if it could even deliver it</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~35 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">1,500W heater on a 1,024Wh power station</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">17-22 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">45W 12V heated blanket on the same station</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Once You Accept the Physics</h3>

          <p>
            Once you accept the physics, the shopping gets easy. If you need to heat air, you need fuel, usually propane, with a battery only for the fan. If you need to heat a person, a battery is excellent, because a vest or blanket needs 10 to 60 watts instead of 1,500. And if you have a large power station, it can run a low-watt heater in short bursts or a heated blanket all night. The products above are ranked on that basis.
          </p>
        </div>

