        <div id="tool-battery-heaters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Tool-Battery Propane Heaters: What the Battery Really Does
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Battery Runs the Fan, Propane Makes the Heat</h3>

          <p>
            DEWALT, Milwaukee and RYOBI all sell heaters that slot into their cordless platforms, and the marketing understandably leads with the battery. In every case, the battery is the smallest part of the system. It powers a fan to move heat, an electronic igniter, a control board and sometimes a work light. Propane does all the heating. That is good news for runtime: a fan draws a fraction of what a heating element would, so one tool battery can last a long session.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Radiant vs Forced Air</h3>

          <p>
            The two designs behave very differently. Radiant heaters like the DEWALT DXH12B use a ceramic burner face that glows and warms people and objects in front of it, similar to sunlight. They run at lower output, typically 6,000 to 18,000 BTU, and the indoor-rated versions have an oxygen depletion sensor that shuts the burner off if oxygen in the room falls, plus a tip-over switch. Forced-air heaters like the RYOBI PCL801B and Milwaukee 0801-20 blow a stream of hot combustion air across the space. They produce far more heat, 30,000 to 70,000 BTU, and they are designed for well-ventilated work areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match Your Battery Platform</h3>

          <p>
            If you already own a battery platform, buy the heater that matches it. A heater that uses the batteries in your garage is far more likely to be ready when you need it than one with its own proprietary pack. Our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DEWALT comparison</a> and <a href="/ryobi-vs-dewalt/">RYOBI vs DEWALT breakdown</a> cover the wider ecosystems if you are choosing one from scratch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Makita Is Missing</h3>

          <p>
            Makita owners will notice their brand is missing from the heater cards. In the US, Makita does not currently sell a battery propane heater; its 18V products in this space are fans and the DCJ205Z heated jacket. That jacket is a strong option for Makita users, rated by Makita for many hours of heat on a 6.0Ah LXT battery, and it belongs in the same category as the ORORO vest on this list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan Your Propane</h3>

          <p>
            Fuel planning matters as much as battery planning. A 1 lb propane cylinder holds about 21,600 BTU, so a radiant heater on its 12,000 BTU setting burns through one in under two hours. A 20 lb tank holds roughly 430,000 BTU, enough for about 36 hours at 12,000 BTU or about 7 hours at 60,000 BTU. For anything beyond an afternoon, a hose and a 20 lb tank are cheaper and less wasteful than stacks of disposable cylinders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Running a Heater From a Power Station
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Power Station Can Run</h3>

          <p>
            A portable power station is the only way to run a true electric heater from a battery, and it is worth understanding exactly what you get. The formula is simple: usable watt-hours divided by the heater's draw equals hours of runtime. Use about 85 percent of the rated capacity for AC loads, because the inverter loses some energy as heat, and closer to 90 percent for 12V loads that skip the inverter.
          </p>

