          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fine for an Evening, Not a Long Outage</h3>

          <p>
            The table makes the conclusion obvious. A power station can absolutely run a space heater, and if your outage lasts one evening it can take the chill off a bedroom before you climb into bed. But it is the worst possible use of stored energy in a long outage. The same battery that buys 35 minutes of room heat buys a full night of warmth under a blanket. Our deep dive on <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-space-heater/">whether a portable power station can run a space heater</a> walks through the surge, inverter and wattage details.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Do Run a Heater From a Station</h3>

          <p>
            If you do plan to run a heater from a station, use a small, low-wattage model with a thermostat and an adjustable setting, put it in the smallest room you can close off, and run it in bursts rather than continuously. Make sure the station's continuous AC rating comfortably exceeds the heater's draw, since a 1,500W heater on a 1,500W station will trip it. A unit like the DELTA 2 with 1,800W continuous has headroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size the Station to the Load List</h3>

          <p>
            Size the station to the load list, not to the heater. Our <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">power station sizing guide</a> shows how to add up a fridge, phones, lights and a blanket so heat does not starve the essentials, and the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations roundup</a> ranks the units worth buying at each capacity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="heat-the-person" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Heat the Person, Not the Room
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Heat the People, Not the House</h3>

          <p>
            The single best idea on this page is old: if you cannot heat the house, heat the people in it. Humans did this for centuries with layers, quilts and bed warmers, and modern batteries make it effortless. A heated vest uses 5 to 15 watts. A 12V blanket uses around 45. A household electric blanket on a low setting is often 40 to 60 watts once it has warmed up. Compare that with 1,500 watts for a heater that warms air which then leaks out through windows and walls.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Riding Out an Outage at 50 to 55 Degrees</h3>

          <p>
            In practice, a household can ride out a multi-day winter outage at an indoor temperature of 50 to 55 degrees with surprisingly little misery if everyone has personal heat. Put on a base layer, a heated vest and a sweater during the day. Sleep under a heated blanket with a regular comforter on top, which traps the heat. Use rechargeable hand warmers in pockets and sleeping bags. Gather everyone in one room with the door closed and towels at the gaps, ideally a room on the sunny side of the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Save Your Battery Reserve</h3>

          <p>
            This approach also keeps your battery reserve for the things that matter. A 1kWh station can recharge every vest and hand warmer in the house many times, run blankets overnight and still have energy left for phones, a router and lights. Our <a href="/best-portable-charger-for-power-outages/">portable chargers for power outages guide</a> covers the small batteries that keep wearables topped up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Limit: It Does Not Protect the House</h3>

          <p>
            Personal heat has one real limit: it does not protect the house. Pipes can freeze when indoor temperatures fall into the 30s, and pets, houseplants and older relatives with circulation problems may need room heat. For long outages in very cold climates, combine personal heat with an indoor-rated propane heater used safely, or with a generator large enough to run a gas furnace blower. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generators for power outages guide</a> covers that route.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety: Propane, Carbon Monoxide and Lithium
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane and Carbon Monoxide</h3>

          <p>
            Any heater that burns propane produces carbon monoxide, and carbon monoxide kills people every winter, often during power outages. Indoor-rated radiant heaters burn cleanly and carry oxygen depletion sensors, but an ODS measures oxygen, not carbon monoxide, and it is not a substitute for a CO alarm. If you run a propane heater anywhere near people, you need a battery-powered or battery-backup carbon monoxide alarm in the same room and outside sleeping areas.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Forced-air propane heaters never go in living spaces.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">RYOBI and Milwaukee style torpedo heaters are built for ventilated job sites and open garages. Never run one in a closed garage attached to a house, a basement, a bedroom, a tent or a camper. Never use a grill, camp stove or generator indoors for heat, ever.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ventilate Even Indoor-Rated Heaters</h3>

          <p>
            Even indoor-rated radiant heaters need fresh air. Crack a window an inch or two on the side of the room away from the heater, keep at least three feet of clearance from bedding, curtains and furniture, and never leave one running while you sleep. Store and swap propane cylinders outdoors, and check the regulator and hose connections with soapy water if you smell gas. Some jurisdictions restrict indoor propane heaters entirely, so follow the label for where you live.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lithium and Wearable Risks</h3>

          <p>
            Battery heat has its own, smaller risks. Buy wearables and blankets from established brands with real certifications, since cheap heated clothing with unbranded cells is where lithium fires happen. Do not charge vest batteries under pillows or on beds, and do not sleep with a heating pad or blanket that lacks an automatic shutoff. For power stations, LiFePO4 chemistry is the safest choice for indoor use; our guide to <a href="/are-portable-power-stations-safe-indoors/">whether power stations are safe indoors</a> covers placement and ventilation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Be Wary of Overnight Safety Claims</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, be suspicious of any small battery heater sold as safe for a sleeping child's room or a closed car overnight. Electric personal heat is safe when it is a certified blanket or garment used as directed. Combustion heat is never safe in a closed car, and a cheap gadget that claims to warm a car interior from a battery is either too weak to matter or dangerous to the battery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-choose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Choose by Situation
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For a Winter Power Outage at Home</h3>

          <p>
            For a winter power outage at home, start with personal heat: one heated vest per adult, a heated blanket per bed and a power station to keep them charged. Add an indoor-rated radiant propane heater like the DEWALT DXH12B or a Mr. Heater Buddy for room heat in short, supervised sessions, with a carbon monoxide alarm and a cracked window. That combination keeps a family comfortable without betting everything on fuel or on one big battery.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For a Garage or Workshop</h3>

          <p>
            For a garage or workshop, a tool-battery forced-air heater matched to your platform is the fastest heat per dollar, used with the big door cracked. If you work out there every weekend, a permanently installed vented or electric garage heater is cheaper to run and safer over time, and our <a href="/best-garage-heater/">best garage heaters guide</a> compares them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For a Car, Truck or Road Trip</h3>

          <p>
            For a car, truck or road trip, a 12V heated blanket plugged into the car socket while the engine runs, plus hand warmers and a heated vest, covers a breakdown or a cold commute. Keep a power station in the trunk on long winter drives if you want blanket heat with the engine off. For camping, skip room heat in a tent entirely. A heated blanket on a power station, a good sleeping bag and hand warmers are safer and lighter than any propane option, and our <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">camping power station guide</a> has the right batteries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For a Desk or Home Office</h3>

          <p>
            For a desk or home office in a cold room, a heated vest or a small electric throw beats a space heater on both comfort and energy cost even when the grid is up. It costs pennies a day to run and keeps you warm without heating an empty room around you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="avoid" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Battery Heaters Not Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Generic Fan-Box Battery Heaters</h3>

          <p>
            Search any marketplace for a battery space heater and you will find dozens of small boxes with fans, sold under rotating brand names, promising to warm a room on a rechargeable battery. Look at the specs and you will find outputs of 10 to 60 watts, or no wattage listed at all. A 50W heater produces about one thirtieth of the heat of a standard space heater. It will warm your hands if you hold them in front of it, and that is all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Impossible Spec Combinations</h3>

          <p>
            Be equally wary of products that list impossible combinations, such as a 1,500W heater with a built-in battery the size of a lunchbox and eight hours of runtime. As the math above shows, that would require a battery heavier than the heater. Listings like that are either misleading or describing a corded heater with a battery-powered remote.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">USB and Novelty Heaters</h3>

          <p>
            USB-powered heaters, desk heaters that plug into a laptop and heated mugs with fans are novelty items. They draw 5 to 20 watts, which is fine for a USB hand warmer pressed against your skin but useless as air heat. If a product calls itself a battery room heater and costs less than a tank of propane, assume it is one of these.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Is Worth Your Money</h3>

          <p>
            The products worth your money are the ones that are honest about where the heat comes from: propane heaters that use a battery for the fan, wearables and blankets that heat a person directly, and power stations large enough to run a heater briefly. Buy from brands with real support, certifications and replacement batteries, and the result is heat that works when the power does not.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a space heater that runs on a rechargeable battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not one that heats a room for long on battery alone. A 1,500W heater would drain a large tool battery in about five minutes. Heaters sold as battery powered either use the battery for a fan while propane makes the heat, like the DEWALT DXH12B, or produce only 10 to 60 watts. A power station can run a real heater briefly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a power station run a space heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide usable watt-hours by the heater's wattage. A 1,024Wh station with about 870Wh usable runs a 1,500W heater for roughly 35 minutes and a 750W setting for about 70 minutes. A 3,840Wh unit manages a little over two hours on high. That is fine for short bursts, not overnight heat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best battery powered heater for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most households, a mix: heated vests and a heated blanket for each person, powered by a power station, plus an indoor-rated radiant propane heater like the DEWALT DXH12B or a Mr. Heater Buddy for short, supervised room heat with a carbon monoxide alarm and a cracked window. That keeps people warm without depending on one fuel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the DEWALT cordless propane heater safe indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It has an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over shutoff and is sold for indoor use in most of the US, but some places, including Massachusetts and Canada, restrict it to outdoor use. Indoors, keep a window cracked, keep three feet of clearance, never sleep with it running and always use a carbon monoxide alarm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Milwaukee M18 heater work without propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The Milwaukee M18 forced-air heater, like the RYOBI and DEWALT models, uses its battery only for the fan, ignition and controls. Propane supplies all of the heat. Without a propane tank connected, it will just blow room-temperature air. It is also designed for ventilated work areas, not living spaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a heated vest battery last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ORORO rates most of its vests at about 3 hours on high, 5 to 6 hours on medium and 8 to 10 hours on low with the included 7.4V battery, which holds roughly 35Wh. Cold weather and using all zones on high shorten that. A second battery doubles runtime, and a power station can recharge a pack many times.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 12V heated blanket run from a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the most efficient ways to stay warm on battery. Plug it into the station's 12V car port to skip inverter losses. Owners commonly measure about 40 to 50 watts, so a 1,024Wh station runs one for roughly 17 to 22 hours, compared with about 35 minutes for a space heater.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a tiny battery heater for room heat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A rechargeable heater rated at 20 to 60 watts cannot warm a room no matter what the photos show. Standard space heaters draw 1,500 watts for a reason. If a battery heater lacks a propane tank or a large power station behind it, treat it as a hand warmer, not a room heater.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a forced-air propane heater indoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Torpedo-style heaters from tool brands are for ventilated job sites and open garages. In a closed room they can build carbon monoxide quickly and consume oxygen. For indoor room heat, use only an indoor-rated radiant heater with an oxygen depletion sensor, a cracked window and a carbon monoxide alarm.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spending power station capacity on a space heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,024Wh station runs a 1,500W heater for about 35 minutes, or a 45W heated blanket for most of a day. In a long outage, burning your battery on room heat leaves nothing for phones, lights or the fridge. Heat people first, and use the heater only in short bursts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the carbon monoxide alarm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oxygen depletion sensor shuts a propane heater off when oxygen drops, but it does not detect carbon monoxide. Any time a fuel-burning heater runs near people, place a battery-powered or battery-backup CO alarm in the same room and outside bedrooms, and test it before the season starts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a heater that fits no battery you own</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A propane heater with a battery fan is only useful if a charged battery is ready when the power fails. Buy the DEWALT, RYOBI or Milwaukee heater that matches the tools in your garage, and keep at least one battery on a charger or topped up through winter.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

